          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01TyLZQk6QbCRVLumdnirpwk",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval combining sparse and dense search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One authoritative source; corroboration on adoption patterns pending.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (such as BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via **reciprocal rank fusion**, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity. This approach consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent, and has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n### Components\\n\\n- **Sparse lexical search** (e.g., BM25): Excels at exact term matching and rare identifiers\\n- **Dense vector search**: Captures semantic similarity and intent\\n- **Reciprocal rank fusion**: Merges ranked result sets robustly\\n\\n### Characteristics\\n\\nHybrid retrieval is particularly effective when queries contain:\\n- Rare identifiers that lexical search handles well\\n- Natural language intent that semantic search captures\\n- Mixed information needs requiring both precision and recall\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Evaluation source: hybrid retrieval strategies in RAG systems (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-02)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
